 {
  "id": 13,
  "platform": "NES",
  "game": "Zelda II: The Adventure of Link",
  "target": "Palace Temples",
  "url": "https://strategywiki.org/wiki/Zelda_II:_The_Adventure_of_Link/Walkthrough",
  "section_count": 3,
  "description": "Palace Temples, Zelda II: The Adventure of Link (NES). In its time it was voted \"Best Overall Game\" in the Nintendo Power Awards '88, was ranked in Nintendo Power's Top 5 games from their January/February 1989 through July/August 1990 issues, and stayed consistently in their Top 30 (later Top 20) NES Titles from the game's introduction in the September/October 1988 issue until the NES Top 20 was phased out in January 1995.",
  "qwen_prompt": "Generate a fully interactive 3D WebGL Three.js world representing 'Palace Temples' from Zelda II: The Adventure of Link (NES). The attached reference imagery is the primary guide: match its layout, landmarks, colors and mood. World brief: Palace Temples, Zelda II: The Adventure of Link (NES). In its time it was voted \"Best Overall Game\" in the Nintendo Power Awards '88, was ranked in Nintendo Power's Top 5 games from their January/February 1989 through July/August 1990 issues, and stayed consistently in their Top 30 (later Top 20) NES Titles from the game's introduction in the September/October 1988 issue until the NES Top 20 was phased out in January 1995. Include lighting, fog, textures/materials, animated environmental elements, and mouse/touch orbit camera controls.",
  "description_raw": "Zelda II: The Adventure of Linkis anaction-adventuregame developed and published byNintendofor theNES, and the second installment inThe Legend of Zeldaseries. It was originally released in Japan on theFamicom Disk Systemless than a year after the release of the originalLegend of Zelda. Nintendo released Zelda II in Japan on January 14,1987, seven months before the United States saw the release of the first Zelda title. It was the second best selling Famicom game released in 1987, selling approximately 1,610,000 copies in its lifetime (roughly two thirds as many sales asDragon Warrior II). Nintendo released Zelda II in North America in 1988, two years after its initial release in Japan, converting the game from its initial Famicom Disk System format to the NES cartridge. The Adventure of Link's emphasis on side-scrolling and RPG-style elements was a significant departure from its predeces",
  "brief": "Palace Temples, Zelda II: The Adventure of Link (NES). In its time it was voted \"Best Overall Game\" in the Nintendo Power Awards '88, was ranked in Nintendo Power's Top 5 games from their January/February 1989 through July/August 1990 issues, and stayed consistently in their Top 30 (later Top 20) NES Titles from the game's introduction in the September/October 1988 issue until the NES Top 20 was phased out in January 1995.",
  "page_title": "Zelda II: The Adventure of Link",
  "image_url": "https://cdn.wikimg.net/en/strategywiki/images/4/4d/Zelda2fds.jpg",
  "image_kind": "screenshot",
  "image_urls": [
   "https://cdn.wikimg.net/en/strategywiki/images/4/4d/Zelda2fds.jpg"
  ],
  "image_kinds": [
   "screenshot"
  ]
 },
 {
  "id": 14,
  "platform": "NES",
  "game": "Metroid",
  "target": "Norfair Volcanic Caverns",
  "url": "https://strategywiki.org/wiki/Metroid/Walkthrough",
  "section_count": 2,
  "description": "Norfair Volcanic Caverns, Metroid (NES). Norfair is a region of lava pools and glass bubbles. In Norfair, many of the pathways which used to be clear have caved in or been filled by cooled lava. However, narrow pathways still exist inside the rubble.",
  "qwen_prompt": "Generate a fully interactive 3D WebGL Three.js world representing 'Norfair Volcanic Caverns' from Metroid (NES). The attached reference imagery is the primary guide: match its layout, landmarks, colors and mood. World brief: Norfair Volcanic Caverns, Metroid (NES). Norfair is a region of lava pools and glass bubbles. In Norfair, many of the pathways which used to be clear have caved in or been filled by cooled lava. However, narrow pathways still exist inside the rubble. Include lighting, fog, textures/materials, animated environmental elements, and mouse/touch orbit camera controls.",
  "description_raw": "Norfair is a region of lava pools and glass bubbles. This is where you will find the Screw Attack, Wave Beam, and High Jump boots, plus extra missiles and energy tanks. Take the Ice Beam with you and freeze enemies for platforms in order to jump over high walls. In Norfair, many of the pathways which used to be clear have caved in or been filled by cooled lava. However, narrow pathways still exist inside the rubble. Never assume you've found a dead end until you've fully explored every wall, floor, and ceiling. Norfair is also along the path to Ridley's Hideout.",
  "brief": "Norfair Volcanic Caverns, Metroid (NES). Norfair is a region of lava pools and glass bubbles. In Norfair, many of the pathways which used to be clear have caved in or been filled by cooled lava. However, narrow pathways still exist inside the rubble.",
