 {
  "id": 15,
  "platform": "NES",
  "game": "Castlevania",
  "target": "Clock Tower",
  "url": "https://strategywiki.org/wiki/Castlevania/Walkthrough",
  "section_count": 5,
  "description": "Clock Tower, Castlevania (NES). By breaking candelabra, torches, and fake walls located throughout the castle, Simon collects hearts, which can then be used to activate whateversub-weaponhe possesses at that point. Castlevania, known in Japan asAkumajou Dracula(translated as \"Demon Castle Dracula\"), is a game developed and published byKonamifor theFamicom Disk Systemin Japan in September of1986. This is the first game in theCastlevaniaseries.",
  "qwen_prompt": "Generate a fully interactive 3D WebGL Three.js world representing 'Clock Tower' from Castlevania (NES). The attached reference imagery is the primary guide: match its layout, landmarks, colors and mood. World brief: Clock Tower, Castlevania (NES). By breaking candelabra, torches, and fake walls located throughout the castle, Simon collects hearts, which can then be used to activate whateversub-weaponhe possesses at that point. Castlevania, known in Japan asAkumajou Dracula(translated as \"Demon Castle Dracula\"), is a game developed and published byKonamifor theFamicom Disk Systemin Japan in September of1986. This is the first game in theCastlevaniaseries. Include lighting, fog, textures/materials, animated environmental elements, and mouse/touch orbit camera controls.",
  "description_raw": "This is the first game in theCastlevaniaseries. For other games in the series see theCastlevania category. Castlevania, known in Japan asAkumajou Dracula(translated as \"Demon Castle Dracula\"), is a game developed and published byKonamifor theFamicom Disk Systemin Japan in September of1986. In May 1987 it was ported to cartridge format and released in North America for theNESfollowed by a European release in 1988, and then once again in Japan in 1993 as a cartridge. It is the first game in theCastlevania franchise, but the seventh chronological installment of the franchise in the current canon. Castlevaniais a typical platform game of the 8-bit era: the game comprises six levels, which are played through in a strictly linear progression. The player controlsSimon Belmont, whose primary mode of attack is via his whip, which can be upgraded by obtaining special items throughout the course of",
  "brief": "Clock Tower, Castlevania (NES). By breaking candelabra, torches, and fake walls located throughout the castle, Simon collects hearts, which can then be used to activate whateversub-weaponhe possesses at that point. Castlevania, known in Japan asAkumajou Dracula(translated as \"Demon Castle Dracula\"), is a game developed and published byKonamifor theFamicom Disk Systemin Japan in September of1986. This is the first game in theCastlevaniaseries.",
  "page_title": "Castlevania",
  "image_url": null,
  "image_kind": null,
  "image_urls": [],
  "image_kinds": []
