  "id": 98,
  "platform": "Arcade",
  "game": "Defender",
  "target": "Cylindrical City Horizon",
  "url": "https://strategywiki.org/wiki/Defender",
  "section_count": 3,
  "description": "Cylindrical City Horizon, Defender (Arcade). The break-neck pace of the action made the game an easy choice for Atari to buy the home licensing rights. Atarisoft handled the conversion of the game to several other home console and computer platforms. This is the first game in theDefenderseries.",
  "qwen_prompt": "Generate a fully interactive 3D WebGL Three.js world representing 'Cylindrical City Horizon' from Defender (Arcade). The attached reference imagery is the primary guide: match its layout, landmarks, colors and mood. World brief: Cylindrical City Horizon, Defender (Arcade). The break-neck pace of the action made the game an easy choice for Atari to buy the home licensing rights. Atarisoft handled the conversion of the game to several other home console and computer platforms. This is the first game in theDefenderseries. Include lighting, fog, textures/materials, animated environmental elements, and mouse/touch orbit camera controls.",
  "description_raw": "This is the first game in theDefenderseries. For other games in the series see theDefender category. Defenderis considered one of the most challenging arcade games ever made. One look at the controls was enough to send many players in search of a simpler game. But those who were brave enough to attempt it found a compelling and addictive game, with an incredibly rich level of strategy. Defender was the brain child of early (and continuing) game developer Eugene Jarvis. Eugene wished to make a video game that immersed the player more than the myriad of Space Invader clones that flooded the markets, and by many experts' accounts, he succeeded. The break-neck pace of the action made the game an easy choice for Atari to buy the home licensing rights. They brought a conversion to the 2600 that was generally well received, despite its shortcomings. But the version created for the 8-bit family ",
  "brief": "Cylindrical City Horizon, Defender (Arcade). The break-neck pace of the action made the game an easy choice for Atari to buy the home licensing rights. Atarisoft handled the conversion of the game to several other home console and computer platforms. This is the first game in theDefenderseries.",
  "page_title": "Defender",
  "image_url": null,
