 {
  "id": 132,
  "platform": "NES",
  "game": "Legacy of the Wizard",
  "target": "Underworld Labyrinth",
  "url": "https://strategywiki.org/wiki/Legacy_of_the_Wizard/Gameplay",
  "page_title": "Legacy of the Wizard",
  "section_count": 5,
  "description": "Underworld Labyrinth, Legacy of the Wizard (NES). The placement of key items has also been shifted around in the MSX2 version. The MSX and MSX2 versions differ in some key ways. The MSX2 graphics are improved and updated over the MSX version.",
  "qwen_prompt": "Generate a fully interactive 3D WebGL Three.js world representing 'Underworld Labyrinth' from Legacy of the Wizard (NES). The attached reference imagery is the primary guide: match its layout, landmarks, colors and mood. World brief: Underworld Labyrinth, Legacy of the Wizard (NES). The placement of key items has also been shifted around in the MSX2 version. The MSX and MSX2 versions differ in some key ways. The MSX2 graphics are improved and updated over the MSX version. Include lighting, fog, textures/materials, animated environmental elements, and mouse/touch orbit camera controls.",
  "description_raw": "The game is considered to be quite difficult as the game presents a rather sprawling dungeon/labyrinth to the player with little to no explanation or direction to follow. Only trial and error can guide the player in determining what steps to take to complete the game. The MSX and MSX2 versions differ in some key ways. The MSX2 graphics are improved and updated over the MSX version. The placement of key items has also been shifted around in the MSX2 version. The daughter's portion of the map is almost completely redesigned. The Famicom/NES version of the game appears to combine the original level arrangement of the MSX version with the improved graphics of the MSX2 version, while adding smooth scrolling to the presentation, as the MSX systems were known to have poor scrolling capabilities.",
  "brief": "Underworld Labyrinth, Legacy of the Wizard (NES). The placement of key items has also been shifted around in the MSX2 version. The MSX and MSX2 versions differ in some key ways. The MSX2 graphics are improved and updated over the MSX version.",
  "image_url": null,
  "image_kind": null,
  "image_urls": [],
  "image_kinds": []
 },
 {
  "id": 133,
  "platform": "NES",
  "game": "Battle City",
  "target": "Brick & Steel Tank Arena",
  "url": "https://strategywiki.org/wiki/Battle_City/Walkthrough",
  "page_title": "Battle City",
  "section_count": 7,
  "description": "Brick & Steel Tank Arena, Battle City (NES). It was also released for the Japanese only Fujitsu FM-77 home computer. Battle Cityis a Famicom game thatNamcomade as an improvement over an older arcade title of theirs:Tank Battalion. While Tank Battalion was very blocky with minimal graphics and rough animation, Battle City spruced things up a bit with more graphical variation, an assortment of enemy tanks, power-ups, and smooth sprite movement.",
  "qwen_prompt": "Generate a fully interactive 3D WebGL Three.js world representing 'Brick & Steel Tank Arena' from Battle City (NES). The attached reference imagery is the primary guide: match its layout, landmarks, colors and mood. World brief: Brick & Steel Tank Arena, Battle City (NES). It was also released for the Japanese only Fujitsu FM-77 home computer. Battle Cityis a Famicom game thatNamcomade as an improvement over an older arcade title of theirs:Tank Battalion. While Tank Battalion was very blocky with minimal graphics and rough animation, Battle City spruced things up a bit with more graphical variation, an assortment of enemy tanks, power-ups, and smooth sprite movement. Include lighting, fog, textures/materials, animated environmental elements, and mouse/touch orbit camera controls.",
  "description_raw": "Battle Cityis a Famicom game thatNamcomade as an improvement over an older arcade title of theirs:Tank Battalion. While Tank Battalion was very blocky with minimal graphics and rough animation, Battle City spruced things up a bit with more graphical variation, an assortment of enemy tanks, power-ups, and smooth sprite movement. Namco did this once before withWarpman, which was an improved version of Warp-Warp. After being released for the Famicom, it was converted into an arcade game calledVs. Battle Cityusing the Nintendo Vs. arcade technology. In1991, Nova ported the game to theGame Boy, adding 65 more stages to bring the stage count up to 100. However, the reduced screen size resulted in only a portion of the maze being shown so a radar was incorporated into the game. Namco later included the game in their Super Game Boy compatible compilationNamco Gallery Vol. 1. It was also released",
  "brief": "Brick & Steel Tank Arena, Battle City (NES). It was also released for the Japanese only Fujitsu FM-77 home computer. Battle Cityis a Famicom game thatNamcomade as an improvement over an older arcade title of theirs:Tank Battalion. While Tank Battalion was very blocky with minimal graphics and rough animation, Battle City spruced things up a bit with more graphical variation, an assortment of enemy tanks, power-ups, and smooth sprite movement.",
  "image_url": null,
  "image_kind": null,
  "image_urls": [],
  "image_kinds": []
 },
