 {
  "id": 360,
  "platform": "Sega Genesis / Master System / Game Gear",
  "game": "Twin Cobra",
  "target": "Island Naval Refueling Docks",
  "url": "https://strategywiki.org/wiki/Twin_Cobra",
  "page_title": "Twin Cobra",
  "section_count": 1,
  "description": "Island Naval Refueling Docks, Twin Cobra (Sega Genesis / Master System / Game Gear). It is much harder than the other home versions as it penalizes much more harshly on continue points. At the end of each stage, and occasionally partway through, is a boss. The game was met with mixed response from magazines, specifically the home versions.",
  "qwen_prompt": "Generate a fully interactive 3D WebGL Three.js world representing 'Island Naval Refueling Docks' from Twin Cobra (Sega Genesis / Master System / Game Gear). The attached reference imagery is the primary guide: match its layout, landmarks, colors and mood. World brief: Island Naval Refueling Docks, Twin Cobra (Sega Genesis / Master System / Game Gear). It is much harder than the other home versions as it penalizes much more harshly on continue points. At the end of each stage, and occasionally partway through, is a boss. The game was met with mixed response from magazines, specifically the home versions. Include lighting, fog, textures/materials, animated environmental elements, and mouse/touch orbit camera controls.",
  "description_raw": "Twin Cobra, is a vertically scrollingshooterdeveloped byToaplanand released in arcades in1987byTaitoin Japan and Europe, then in North America byRomstar. It is a sequel to the 1985 arcade gameTiger-HeliControlling the titular attack helicopter, the players must fight endless waves of military vehicles while avoiding collision with their projectiles and other obstacles. It was the fourth shoot 'em up game from Toaplan, and their tenth video game overall. The game is known asKyuukyoku Tiger(究極タイガー,Kyuukyoku Tiger?lit. Ultimate Tiger)in Japan, which continues the tradition set by Tiger-Heli to include the word \"Tiger\" in the title of each game in the series. Subsequent Japan-only sequels would use the same title. The game was originally released in arcades, but subsequently received aFamicom/NESversion, aPC Engineversion (which never saw a TurboGrafx-16 equivalent) and eventually a version ",
  "brief": "Island Naval Refueling Docks, Twin Cobra (Sega Genesis / Master System / Game Gear). It is much harder than the other home versions as it penalizes much more harshly on continue points. At the end of each stage, and occasionally partway through, is a boss. The game was met with mixed response from magazines, specifically the home versions.",
  "image_url": null,
  "image_kind": null,
  "image_urls": [],
  "image_kinds": []
 },
 {
  "id": 361,
  "platform": "Sega Genesis / Master System / Game Gear",
  "game": "Zero Wing",
  "target": "Alien Industrial Megaship Corridors",
  "url": "https://strategywiki.org/wiki/Zero_Wing/Gameplay",
  "page_title": "Zero Wing",
  "section_count": 2,
  "description": "Alien Industrial Megaship Corridors, Zero Wing (Sega Genesis / Master System / Game Gear). The game features eight levels, each with different style and enemies and a few minutes long. Zero Wing(ゼロウィング,Zero Wing?)is a1989side-scrollingshoot 'em uparcadegame developed byToaplanand published byNamcoin Japan andWilliamsin the US.",
  "qwen_prompt": "Generate a fully interactive 3D WebGL Three.js world representing 'Alien Industrial Megaship Corridors' from Zero Wing (Sega Genesis / Master System / Game Gear). The attached reference imagery is the primary guide: match its layout, landmarks, colors and mood. World brief: Alien Industrial Megaship Corridors, Zero Wing (Sega Genesis / Master System / Game Gear). The game features eight levels, each with different style and enemies and a few minutes long. Zero Wing(ゼロウィング,Zero Wing?)is a1989side-scrollingshoot 'em uparcadegame developed byToaplanand published byNamcoin Japan andWilliamsin the US. Include lighting, fog, textures/materials, animated environmental elements, and mouse/touch orbit camera controls.",
  "description_raw": "Zero Wing(ゼロウィング,Zero Wing?)is a1989side-scrollingshoot 'em uparcadegame developed byToaplanand published byNamcoin Japan andWilliamsin the US. It enjoyed a degree of success in arcades and was subsequently ported to theSega Mega Driveby Toaplan on May 31,1991in Japan, and bySegaduring the following year in Europe, followed by aPC Engine CD-ROMrelease byNaxat Softon September 18,1992only in Japan. The player is a lone hero who will save the universe from an evil force. As with other scrolling shooters, the aim of the game is to shoot all enemies that appear on screen and avoid getting obliterated by enemy fire, crashing into enemies or into foreground scenery. There are mid-level and end-of-level boss enemies that stay with the player until they are defeated. The game features eight levels, each with different style and enemies and a few minutes long.",
  "brief": "Alien Industrial Megaship Corridors, Zero Wing (Sega Genesis / Master System / Game Gear). The game features eight levels, each with different style and enemies and a few minutes long. Zero Wing(ゼロウィング,Zero Wing?)is a1989side-scrollingshoot 'em uparcadegame developed byToaplanand published byNamcoin Japan andWilliamsin the US.",
