  "target": "Bakumatsu Flaming Warehouses & Graveyards",
  "url": "https://strategywiki.org/wiki/The_Last_Blade/Gameplay",
  "page_title": "The Last Blade",
  "section_count": 0,
  "description": "Bakumatsu Flaming Warehouses & Graveyards, The Last Blade (Arcade). It was also ported to several home systems. This is the first game in theThe Last Bladeseries. For other games in the series see theThe Last Blade category.",
  "qwen_prompt": "Generate a fully interactive 3D WebGL Three.js world representing 'Bakumatsu Flaming Warehouses & Graveyards' from The Last Blade (Arcade). The attached reference imagery is the primary guide: match its layout, landmarks, colors and mood. World brief: Bakumatsu Flaming Warehouses & Graveyards, The Last Blade (Arcade). It was also ported to several home systems. This is the first game in theThe Last Bladeseries. For other games in the series see theThe Last Blade category. Include lighting, fog, textures/materials, animated environmental elements, and mouse/touch orbit camera controls.",
  "description_raw": "This is the first game in theThe Last Bladeseries. For other games in the series see theThe Last Blade category. The Last Blade, released in Japan asBakumatsu Rouman: Gekka no Kenshi(幕末浪漫 月華の剣士,Bakumatsu Rouman: Gekka no Kenshi?), is a fighting game developed and released bySNKfor theNeo Geoin1997. It was also ported to several home systems. The Last Blade and its sequel,The Last Blade 2, are considered by some to be two of the most accomplished games available for the Neo Geo. The game takes place during the Bakumatsu era in Japan, and incorporates various elements of Japanese mythology (with a heavy emphasis on the symbology of the Four Symbols). As such, the background music generally incorporates synthesized instruments simulating a sound appropriate to the 19th century setting, in a Western classical, pseudo-Romantic style (unusual for a fighting game).",
  "brief": "Bakumatsu Flaming Warehouses & Graveyards, The Last Blade (Arcade). It was also ported to several home systems. This is the first game in theThe Last Bladeseries. For other games in the series see theThe Last Blade category.",
  "image_url": null,
  "image_kind": null,
  "image_urls": [],
  "image_kinds": []
 },
 {
  "id": 399,
  "platform": "Arcade",
  "game": "Garou: Mark of the Wolves",
  "target": "Second South City Train Stations",
  "url": "https://strategywiki.org/wiki/Garou:_Mark_of_the_Wolves/Gameplay",
  "page_title": "Garou: Mark of the Wolves",
  "section_count": 16,
  "description": "Second South City Train Stations, Garou: Mark of the Wolves (Arcade). Garou: Mark of the Wolves, known asFatal Fury: Mark of the Wolveson U.S.Dreamcast, is a1999fightinggame produced bySNK, originally for theNeo Geosystem. It is the ninth and final game in theFatal Furyseries, set ten years after the death of Geese Howard inReal Bout Fatal Fury. Many of these characters reference older characters however.",
  "qwen_prompt": "Generate a fully interactive 3D WebGL Three.js world representing 'Second South City Train Stations' from Garou: Mark of the Wolves (Arcade). The attached reference imagery is the primary guide: match its layout, landmarks, colors and mood. World brief: Second South City Train Stations, Garou: Mark of the Wolves (Arcade). Garou: Mark of the Wolves, known asFatal Fury: Mark of the Wolveson U.S.Dreamcast, is a1999fightinggame produced bySNK, originally for theNeo Geosystem. It is the ninth and final game in theFatal Furyseries, set ten years after the death of Geese Howard inReal Bout Fatal Fury. Many of these characters reference older characters however. Include lighting, fog, textures/materials, animated environmental elements, and mouse/touch orbit camera controls.",
  "description_raw": "Garou: Mark of the Wolves, known asFatal Fury: Mark of the Wolveson U.S.Dreamcast, is a1999fightinggame produced bySNK, originally for theNeo Geosystem. It is the ninth and final game in theFatal Furyseries, set ten years after the death of Geese Howard inReal Bout Fatal Fury. With the exception ofTerry Bogard, the game is comprised of an entirely new cast of characters. Many of these characters reference older characters however. It is known for pushing the graphical capabilities of the Neo Geo as well as its well-balanced roster of characters and highly technical gameplay.",
  "brief": "Second South City Train Stations, Garou: Mark of the Wolves (Arcade). Garou: Mark of the Wolves, known asFatal Fury: Mark of the Wolveson U.S.Dreamcast, is a1999fightinggame produced bySNK, originally for theNeo Geosystem. It is the ninth and final game in theFatal Furyseries, set ten years after the death of Geese Howard inReal Bout Fatal Fury. Many of these characters reference older characters however.",
