  "id": 501,
  "platform": "NES / Famicom",
  "game": "Castlevania",
  "target": "Dracula's Gates, Catacombs & Belfry",
  "url": "https://strategywiki.org/wiki/Castlevania/Gameplay",
  "page_title": "Castlevania",
  "section_count": 5,
  "description": "Dracula's Gates, Catacombs & Belfry, Castlevania (NES / Famicom). By breaking candelabra, torches, and fake walls located throughout the castle, Simon collects hearts, which can then be used to activate whateversub-weaponhe possesses at that point. Castlevania, known in Japan asAkumajou Dracula(translated as \"Demon Castle Dracula\"), is a game developed and published byKonamifor theFamicom Disk Systemin Japan in September of1986. This is the first game in theCastlevaniaseries.",
  "qwen_prompt": "Generate a fully interactive 3D WebGL Three.js world representing 'Dracula's Gates, Catacombs & Belfry' from Castlevania (NES / Famicom). The attached reference imagery is the primary guide: match its layout, landmarks, colors and mood. World brief: Dracula's Gates, Catacombs & Belfry, Castlevania (NES / Famicom). By breaking candelabra, torches, and fake walls located throughout the castle, Simon collects hearts, which can then be used to activate whateversub-weaponhe possesses at that point. Castlevania, known in Japan asAkumajou Dracula(translated as \"Demon Castle Dracula\"), is a game developed and published byKonamifor theFamicom Disk Systemin Japan in September of1986. This is the first game in theCastlevaniaseries. Include lighting, fog, textures/materials, animated environmental elements, and mouse/touch orbit camera controls.",
  "description_raw": "This is the first game in theCastlevaniaseries. For other games in the series see theCastlevania category. Castlevania, known in Japan asAkumajou Dracula(translated as \"Demon Castle Dracula\"), is a game developed and published byKonamifor theFamicom Disk Systemin Japan in September of1986. In May 1987 it was ported to cartridge format and released in North America for theNESfollowed by a European release in 1988, and then once again in Japan in 1993 as a cartridge. It is the first game in theCastlevania franchise, but the seventh chronological installment of the franchise in the current canon. Castlevaniais a typical platform game of the 8-bit era: the game comprises six levels, which are played through in a strictly linear progression. The player controlsSimon Belmont, whose primary mode of attack is via his whip, which can be upgraded by obtaining special items throughout the course of",
  "brief": "Dracula's Gates, Catacombs & Belfry, Castlevania (NES / Famicom). By breaking candelabra, torches, and fake walls located throughout the castle, Simon collects hearts, which can then be used to activate whateversub-weaponhe possesses at that point. Castlevania, known in Japan asAkumajou Dracula(translated as \"Demon Castle Dracula\"), is a game developed and published byKonamifor theFamicom Disk Systemin Japan in September of1986. This is the first game in theCastlevaniaseries.",
  "image_url": null,
  "image_kind": null,
  "image_urls": [],
  "image_kinds": []
 },
 {
  "id": 502,
  "platform": "NES / Famicom",
  "game": "Castlevania III: Dracula's Curse",
  "target": "Forest of Madness & Sunken City",
  "url": "https://strategywiki.org/wiki/Castlevania_III:_Dracula%27s_Curse/Gameplay",
  "page_title": "Castlevania III: Dracula's Curse",
  "section_count": 1,
  "description": "Forest of Madness & Sunken City, Castlevania III: Dracula's Curse (NES / Famicom). Devil Castle Dracula Legend), is an action-adventure platform game developed and published byKonamifor theNES. The game departs from the original by offering multiple paths to get from the beginning of the game to Dracula's castle. Castlevania III: Dracula's Curse, released in Japan asAkumajou Dorakula Densetsu(悪魔城伝説,Akumajou Dorakula Densetsu?lit.",
  "qwen_prompt": "Generate a fully interactive 3D WebGL Three.js world representing 'Forest of Madness & Sunken City' from Castlevania III: Dracula's Curse (NES / Famicom). World brief: Forest of Madness & Sunken City, Castlevania III: Dracula's Curse (NES / Famicom). Devil Castle Dracula Legend), is an action-adventure platform game developed and published byKonamifor theNES. The game departs from the original by offering multiple paths to get from the beginning of the game to Dracula's castle. Castlevania III: Dracula's Curse, released in Japan asAkumajou Dorakula Densetsu(悪魔城伝説,Akumajou Dorakula Densetsu?lit. Include lighting, fog, textures/materials, animated environmental elements, and mouse/touch orbit camera controls.",
  "description_raw": "Castlevania III: Dracula's Curse, released in Japan asAkumajou Dorakula Densetsu(悪魔城伝説,Akumajou Dorakula Densetsu?lit. Devil Castle Dracula Legend), is an action-adventure platform game developed and published byKonamifor theNES. It was released in Japan in1989, and in North America in1990, and in Europe byPalcomin1992. It was later released on theVirtual Consolefor theWii,Nintendo 3DS, andWii U. Castlevania III: Dracula's Curse is the third installment in the Castlevania video game series. While theprevious gamecontained more open world exploration, this game returns to the more linear side-scrolling formula established bythe original. It is a prequel to the original Castlevania, set a few centuries before the events of the original game. The game's protagonist is Trevor C. Belmont, an ancestor of the original hero Simon Belmont. The game is chronologically the first game afterLament of",
  "brief": "Forest of Madness & Sunken City, Castlevania III: Dracula's Curse (NES / Famicom). Devil Castle Dracula Legend), is an action-adventure platform game developed and published byKonamifor theNES. The game departs from the original by offering multiple paths to get from the beginning of the game to Dracula's castle. Castlevania III: Dracula's Curse, released in Japan asAkumajou Dorakula Densetsu(悪魔城伝説,Akumajou Dorakula Densetsu?lit."
 },
